- **Accepting an offered credential that cannot address the blocker** — a sudo
  password offered for a task needing no root wastes the user's effort and
  leaves the real blocker standing. Name the missing capability, then the
  concrete fixes.
- **Delegating a blocked task to a peer agent without checking its credentials
  first** — a second agent on the same box shares the same missing token, so
  handing off a push that needs repo scope just moves the failure. Check the
  peer's environment (`which gh`, `GITHUB_TOKEN`, credential store, its own
  auth.json) before offering it as a path; if it has no more access than you,
  say so and do the part you *can* do.
- **Calling a program broken because no window appeared** — triage the
  process table before the display layer, because "not running", "running with
  no window", and "running and blocked" have three different fixes and the
  first two look identical from the user's chair. Read it in this order:
  `pgrep -a <prog>` (a live PID plus a child is the app working), then the
  app's own log/config dir (`~/.local/share/<app>/logs/`, `~/.config/<app>/`),
  and only then suspect the display stack. A counter advancing in the log is
  positive proof of progress, so a missing window is not missing work. Watch
  for the updater that runs as a **detached child** and may never map a window
  at all under Wayland while writing its progress to a log — the log lines are
  the dialog's content going to a file, so the absence of the popup is normal
  and not the symptom. Also expect first-run `ERROR` lines that are pure
  noise (no cached hosts file, no install manifest to verify, a missing
  bootstrap asset) and read them against whether the state they complain about
  *should* exist yet on a clean install; do not report them as failures.
